{"candidates":[{"claim":"Greedy insertion of a 13th channel into optimal 12-input networks cannot yield a network of size < 45, because optimal 12-input networks are structurally rigid, requiring at least 7 additional comparators (yielding 46) when extended to 13 inputs, explaining why search stalls at 45 for de novo 13-input constructions.","counterconditions":["A specific 12-input optimal network can be extended with exactly 6 comparators to sort 13 inputs.","A different insertion strategy bypasses the rigidity and yields a 45-comparator network."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"8a7a7d8f89f48b3e"},{"block":"1194dff78a2795cf"}],"mechanism":"Optimal 12-input networks are maximally packed. Adding a 13th channel requires integrating the new element into the sorted sequence. Due to the straight-line structure, adding a channel needs comparators that interact with the existing fixed structure. Greedy insertion takes at least 7 extra comparators (39+7=46). This structural rigidity prevents 45-comparator extensions from 12-input networks.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-c684127f981a8a8c1d5e0739c804e68436eebc3d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}